### What does acks=all actually promise?

It does not mean all replicas. It means all replicas that are currently in the in-sync replica set (ISR). A follower is in the ISR only while it keeps up with the leader within `replica.lag.time.max.ms`. The ISR can shrink at runtime.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class AcksAllConfig {
    public static Properties create() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.RETRIES_CONFIG, Integer.toString(Integer.MAX_VALUE));

        System.out.println("acks=all: wait for ALL in-sync replicas");
        System.out.println("  WARNING: 'all' means all CURRENTLY in ISR");
        System.out.println("  ISR can shrink to just the leader at runtime");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        create();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
acks=all: wait for ALL in-sync replicas
  WARNING: 'all' means all CURRENTLY in ISR
  ISR can shrink to just the leader at runtime
```

### Where is the durability risk with min.insync.replicas=1?

The dangerous window: followers drop out of ISR (load, GC, restarts), ISR shrinks to just the leader, min.insync.replicas=1 is satisfied, and acks=all is satisfied by the leader alone. The producer gets a success for a message on one broker.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DurabilityRiskScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Replication factor: 3, acks: all, min.insync.replicas: 1");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Normal state:");
        System.out.println("  ISR = {broker-1 (leader), broker-2, broker-3}");
        System.out.println("  acks=all waits for all 3 -- safe");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Dangerous window:");
        System.out.println("  Broker-2 GC pause, broker-3 restarting");
        System.out.println("  ISR = {broker-1 (leader)} -- just ONE");
        System.out.println("  min.insync.replicas=1 is satisfied");
        System.out.println("  acks=all satisfied by leader alone");
        System.out.println("  Producer gets SUCCESS for Rajesh's transfer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Disaster:");
        System.out.println("  Broker-1 disk fails before followers catch up");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh's transfer is GONE FOREVER");
        System.out.println("  Producer already reported SUCCESS");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Replication factor: 3, acks: all, min.insync.replicas: 1

Normal state:
  ISR = {broker-1 (leader), broker-2, broker-3}
  acks=all waits for all 3 -- safe

Dangerous window:
  Broker-2 GC pause, broker-3 restarting
  ISR = {broker-1 (leader)} -- just ONE
  min.insync.replicas=1 is satisfied
  acks=all satisfied by leader alone
  Producer gets SUCCESS for Rajesh's transfer

Disaster:
  Broker-1 disk fails before followers catch up
  Rajesh's transfer is GONE FOREVER
  Producer already reported SUCCESS
```

### How does min.insync.replicas=2 fix this?

With replication factor 3 and `min.insync.replicas=2`, the leader refuses any acks=all write unless at least two replicas are in-sync. Every acknowledged message exists on at least two brokers.

```bash
# Set min.insync.replicas=2 on the settlements topic
kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic settlements \
  --config min.insync.replicas=2 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092

# Verify
kafka-configs.sh --describe --topic settlements \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

```text
Dynamic configs for topic settlements:
  min.insync.replicas=2
```

### What happens when a second replica is unavailable?

With `min.insync.replicas=2`, if only one replica is in-sync, the producer gets `NotEnoughReplicasException` and must retry. You trade a little availability for durability.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class NotEnoughReplicasDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("min.insync.replicas=2, ISR has only 1 replica:");
        System.out.println("  Producer gets: NotEnoughReplicasException");
        System.out.println("  Write REJECTED -- better than silent data loss");
        System.out.println("  Producer retries until ISR recovers to >= 2");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Trade-off:");
        System.out.println("  min.insync.replicas=1: always accepts writes, risks loss");
        System.out.println("  min.insync.replicas=2: may reject writes, guarantees durability");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
min.insync.replicas=2, ISR has only 1 replica:
  Producer gets: NotEnoughReplicasException
  Write REJECTED -- better than silent data loss
  Producer retries until ISR recovers to >= 2

Trade-off:
  min.insync.replicas=1: always accepts writes, risks loss
  min.insync.replicas=2: may reject writes, guarantees durability
```

### How do the three settings form a durability contract?

Durability requires all three settings to agree. Miss any one and you have a silent hole.

| Setting | Purpose | Unsafe Value | Safe Value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Replication factor | Number of copies | 1 | 3 |
| acks | When producer gets success | 0 or 1 | all |
| min.insync.replicas | Floor for ISR size | 1 | 2 |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DurabilityContract {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Durability contract -- all three must agree:");
        System.out.println("  Replication factor = 3 (gives you the copies)");
        System.out.println("  acks = all (makes producer wait for ISR)");
        System.out.println("  min.insync.replicas = 2 (ISR is never just 1 broker)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Result: survives loss of any single broker");
        System.out.println("  without losing acknowledged data");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Durability contract -- all three must agree:
  Replication factor = 3 (gives you the copies)
  acks = all (makes producer wait for ISR)
  min.insync.replicas = 2 (ISR is never just 1 broker)

Result: survives loss of any single broker
  without losing acknowledged data
```

### What about unclean.leader.election.enable?

If `unclean.leader.election.enable=true`, a broker not in the ISR can be elected leader after a failure, which can lose acknowledged data. For banking, keep it false.

```bash
# Disable unclean leader election (should be the default)
kafka-configs.sh --alter --entity-type brokers --entity-default \
  --add-config unclean.leader.election.enable=false \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### What metrics should you monitor?

Alert on under-replicated and at-minimum-ISR partitions. A partition at ISR=1 is a silent durability hole.

```bash
# Check for under-replicated partitions
kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-replicated-partitions \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092

# Monitor ISR count per partition
kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic settlements --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Even RF=3 with min.insync.replicas=2 is not immune to correlated failure (losing two brokers in the same rack). Spread replicas across racks or zones using rack awareness. Alert on the under-minimum-ISR metric because a partition at ISR=1 looks healthy on a dashboard right up until it loses data.
